.TH CPATH 1 "November 2026" "cpath 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpath \- copy canonical absolute paths across local shells and SSH (OSC 52)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpath
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR...]
.br
.B cpath
\fB\-\-set\-host\fR [\fIALIAS\fR]
.br
.B cpath
\fB\-\-clear\-host\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpath
resolves one or more files or directories to their canonical absolute paths and
copies the result to the clipboard. When no
.I PATH
argument is provided, the current working directory (\fB.\fR) is used.
Multiple
.I PATH
arguments are joined with newlines.
.PP
In local sessions,
.B cpath
automatically selects an available native clipboard backend. In SSH sessions, it
emits an OSC 52 escape sequence so modern terminal emulators write directly to
the local clipboard without X11 forwarding.
.SH OPTIONS
Short options may be bundled (for example, \fB\-sd\fR or \fB\-ud\fR). Use
\fB\-\-\fR to end option parsing before paths that begin with a hyphen.
.TP
.B \-d
If a resolved
.I PATH
is not a directory, use its parent directory instead.
.TP
.B \-s
Prepend the resolved host name in \fIhost:/abs/path\fR format.
.TP
.B \-u
Prepend the current user name and host name in \fIuser@host:/abs/path\fR format.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not dereference symbolic links; normalize logical path components instead.
.TP
.B \-p
Print the resulting path(s) to standard output without writing to the clipboard.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-set\-host " [" \fIALIAS\fR ]
Save a persistent host alias for this machine to
.IR ~/.config/cpath/host .
Prompts interactively on
.I /dev/tty
if
.I ALIAS
is omitted.
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-host
Remove the saved persistent host alias and fall back to dynamic host resolution.
.SH HOST RESOLUTION
When \fB\-s\fR or \fB\-u\fR is given,
.B cpath
resolves the host name in the following priority order (highest to lowest):
.IP 1. 4
.B $CPATH_HOST
(one-off environment variable override)
.IP 2. 4
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/cpath/host
(persistent per-machine alias)
.IP 3. 4
.B $LC_SSH_HOST
(forwarded via OpenSSH \fBSetEnv\fR in local \fI~/.ssh/config\fR)
.IP 4. 4
System host name from \fBhostname\fR(1) or \fBuname \-n\fR.
.SH CLIPBOARD BACKENDS
When \fBSSH_CONNECTION\fR, \fBSSH_TTY\fR, or \fBSSH_CLIENT\fR is set,
.B cpath
sends a standard OSC 52 sequence to \fI/dev/tty\fR (or standard output if no
controlling terminal is attached).
.PP
Otherwise, local backends are probed in order:
.BR pbcopy (1)
(macOS),
.BR wl\-copy (1)
(Wayland, requires \fBWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fR),
.BR xclip (1)
and
.BR xsel (1)
(X11, requires \fBDISPLAY\fR), and
.B clip.exe
(Windows WSL).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CPATH_HOST
Highest-priority host alias override for \fB\-s\fR and \fB\-u\fR.
.TP
.B LC_SSH_HOST
Forwarded SSH config host alias used when no saved alias is configured.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for persistent configuration (defaults to \fI~/.config\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/cpath/host
Persistent host alias written by \fBcpath \-\-set\-host\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Copy the current directory path:
.B cpath
.TP
Copy the directory containing a file in \fIhost:path\fR format:
.B cpath \-sd ./config/app.yaml
.TP
Copy a path without dereferencing symbolic links:
.B cpath \-n /var/www/current/index.html
.TP
Print a remote path for command substitution:
.B scp "$(cpath \-sp ./artifact.tar.gz)" ./
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, or non-zero if option parsing, path resolution, or
clipboard writing fails.
